From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 24 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] qapi/ui: add the nvkvm-broker display type

nvkvm's other display options all end in a host window QEMU owns:
-display gtk or -display sdl make QEMU a client of the user's X11 or
Wayland session and pair that with a private EGL context on the NVIDIA
render node.  For a VMM we intend to sandbox that is the wrong shape --
an X11 or Wayland socket inside the sandbox is a far larger and more
powerful interface than the display actually needs, and X11 in
particular has no inter-client isolation at all.

-display nvkvm-broker,socket=PATH replaces all of it with one unix
socket to a privileged display broker (src/broker/).  QEMU connects, is
validated by SO_PEERCRED on the broker's side, and from then on RELAYS
the guest's scanout dma-buf fd -- the one the isolate's stub already
hands it every frame over SCM_RIGHTS -- onward to the broker, which
wraps it in a wl_buffer or an X pixmap and puts it on screen.  Input
comes back as fixed-size packets on the same socket.

The consequence is the point: QEMU imports nothing and touches no
pixels, so in broker mode its display path needs no EGL, no GL, no
libnvidia-eglcore and no /dev/dri/renderD*.  Zero-copy holds end to end
-- one physical allocation seen as the guest's scanout bo, the isolate's
RM object, a host dma-buf and a wl_buffer.

See src/broker/README.md for the threat model and the wire protocol.

Only the QAPI half touches upstream files; the backend itself is a new
file in hw/misc/ (nvkvm_display_relay.c), copied in like the rest of the
device.  Gated on CONFIG_LINUX because SCM_RIGHTS fd relay and dma-buf
are Linux interfaces.
---
diff --git a/qapi/ui.json b/qapi/ui.json
index 460a26b..88089a6 100644
--- a/qapi/ui.json
+++ b/qapi/ui.json
@@ -1470,6 +1470,22 @@
 { 'struct'  : 'DisplaySDL',
   'data'    : { '*grab-mod'   : 'HotKeyMod' } }
 
+##
+# @DisplayNvkvmBroker:
+#
+# Display setup for the nvkvm privileged display broker.
+#
+# @socket: path of the broker's unix socket.  QEMU connects to it and is
+#     validated by SO_PEERCRED before the broker sends anything.  QEMU
+#     then relays the guest's scanout dma-buf fd over SCM_RIGHTS and
+#     receives input events on the same socket.
+#
+# Since: 9.2
+##
+{ 'struct'  : 'DisplayNvkvmBroker',
+  'data'    : { 'socket' : 'str' },
+  'if'      : 'CONFIG_LINUX' }
+
 ##
 # @DisplayType:
 #
@@ -1505,6 +1521,14 @@
 #
 # @dbus: Start a D-Bus service for the display.  (Since 7.0)
 #
+# @nvkvm-broker: Hand the display to the nvkvm display broker over a
+#     unix socket.  QEMU relays the guest's scanout dma-buf fd to the
+#     broker with SCM_RIGHTS and receives keyboard and pointer events
+#     back; the broker owns the window, the compositor connection and
+#     the input grab.  QEMU therefore needs no window-system connection
+#     at all -- no GTK, no SDL, no EGL, and no X11 or Wayland socket
+#     inside QEMU's sandbox.  (Since 9.2)
+#
 # Since: 2.12
 ##
 { 'enum'    : 'DisplayType',
@@ -1517,7 +1541,8 @@
     { 'name': 'curses', 'if': 'CONFIG_CURSES' },
     { 'name': 'cocoa', 'if': 'CONFIG_COCOA' },
     { 'name': 'spice-app', 'if': 'CONFIG_SPICE' },
-    { 'name': 'dbus', 'if': 'CONFIG_DBUS_DISPLAY' }
+    { 'name': 'dbus', 'if': 'CONFIG_DBUS_DISPLAY' },
+    { 'name': 'nvkvm-broker', 'if': 'CONFIG_LINUX' }
   ]
 }
 
@@ -1555,7 +1580,9 @@
       'egl-headless': { 'type': 'DisplayEGLHeadless',
                         'if': 'CONFIG_OPENGL' },
       'dbus': { 'type': 'DisplayDBus', 'if': 'CONFIG_DBUS_DISPLAY' },
-      'sdl': { 'type': 'DisplaySDL', 'if': 'CONFIG_SDL' }
+      'sdl': { 'type': 'DisplaySDL', 'if': 'CONFIG_SDL' },
+      'nvkvm-broker': { 'type': 'DisplayNvkvmBroker',
+                        'if': 'CONFIG_LINUX' }
   }
 }
 
